1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,600
Ruth's check rises every year, and it buys
less than her first one.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:09,600
[curious] At 65, she chose the smaller
annuity check that rises 2 percent a year,

3
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:13,920
and asked one thing: would 2 percent keep
up with prices?

4
00:00:14,933 --> 00:00:17,573
Her twentieth year of checks ended this
August.

5
00:00:17,813 --> 00:00:23,573
We tested that raise against every 20-year
stretch of US prices since 1947,

6
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:27,493
to see how often it kept its buying power,
and how much it usually kept.

7
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:33,060
Here, keeping up means a check still buys
at least what its own first check bought.

8
00:00:33,364 --> 00:00:38,740
Ruth is an illustrative retiree, built on
real US prices,

9
00:00:38,760 --> 00:00:40,980
and today her check falls short of that.

10
00:00:41,156 --> 00:00:44,500
This is US only, and it's history, not a
forecast.

11
00:00:44,633 --> 00:00:47,220
It doesn't say which check to choose.

12
00:00:51,233 --> 00:00:56,113
At 65, an income annuity can pay a bigger
check that never changes,

13
00:00:56,246 --> 00:01:00,033
or a smaller check that rises by the same
percentage every year.

14
00:01:00,233 --> 00:01:04,673
How much smaller the rising check starts
depends on the insurer,

15
00:01:04,693 --> 00:01:06,193
and this video doesn't model it.

16
00:01:06,380 --> 00:01:09,953
So it can't compare the dollars both
checks pay out over a lifetime.

17
00:01:10,113 --> 00:01:14,753
What it measures is buying power: what
each check can buy,

18
00:01:14,797 --> 00:01:17,153
compared with what its own first check
bought.

19
00:01:18,167 --> 00:01:22,087
Ruth took her first rising check in August
2006.

20
00:01:22,207 --> 00:01:27,127
Her reasoning was simple: prices go up,
and her check goes up too.

21
00:01:27,527 --> 00:01:31,127
So what does a raise of 2 percent a year
add up to?

22
00:01:32,133 --> 00:01:38,773
After 20 raises, a check is 48.6 percent
bigger than its first one.

23
00:01:39,013 --> 00:01:46,013
Over Ruth's 20-year stretch, the consumer
price index rose 64.3 percent.

24
00:01:46,213 --> 00:01:50,533
That index is a national average of what
urban households pay for,

25
00:01:50,565 --> 00:01:56,373
from rent to medical care to gasoline, not
any one person's own basket.

26
00:01:57,367 --> 00:02:01,847
Her check grew, but prices grew more, so
today it buys less than her first check

27
00:02:01,907 --> 00:02:02,646
did.

28
00:02:02,647 --> 00:02:06,807
If her first check bought 10 crates of
everything the index tracks,

29
00:02:06,847 --> 00:02:08,807
her check today buys about 9.

30
00:02:09,800 --> 00:02:14,120
Measured against its own first check, the
bigger level check she turned down would

31
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:16,840
buy about 6 in 10 today.

32
00:02:17,240 --> 00:02:20,200
So the raise slowed the loss.

33
00:02:20,312 --> 00:02:23,800
That's measured against each check's own
start,

34
00:02:23,820 --> 00:02:28,680
and the level check started bigger, by an
amount this video doesn't model.

35
00:02:29,667 --> 00:02:35,267
Year by year, Ruth's check bought at least
what her first one did on 12 of its first

36
00:02:35,357 --> 00:02:37,107
15 anniversaries.

37
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:42,307
It slipped under in a few early years,
then climbed back each time.

38
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:49,060
In August 2021, at 80, her check still
bought a little more than her first one

39
00:02:49,140 --> 00:02:50,139
did.

40
00:02:50,140 --> 00:02:54,180
[serious] Then, in a single year, it fell
below,

41
00:02:54,220 --> 00:02:55,940
and it hasn't caught up since.

42
00:02:57,133 --> 00:03:04,133
By August 2022, it bought 94.5 percent of
what her first check did.

43
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:09,820
Measured against its own start, the level
check she turned down fell that far much

44
00:03:09,877 --> 00:03:10,726
sooner.

45
00:03:10,727 --> 00:03:14,860
By year 5, it was already down to about 9
in 10.

46
00:03:15,140 --> 00:03:19,980
Her rising check stands there now, after
20 years.

47
00:03:20,460 --> 00:03:27,420
So was Ruth's stretch unusual, or is this
what a 2 percent raise usually did?

48
00:03:28,933 --> 00:03:34,293
We took every starting month from January
1947 to August 2006,

49
00:03:34,373 --> 00:03:37,493
and followed prices for 20 years after
each one.

50
00:03:37,653 --> 00:03:43,493
That's 715 stretches, and Ruth's is one of
them:

51
00:03:43,513 --> 00:03:44,533
the most recent.

52
00:03:44,913 --> 00:03:51,913
For each one, we asked Ruth's question:
did a check rising 2 percent a year still

53
00:03:52,233 --> 00:03:55,173
buy at least what its first check bought?

54
00:03:55,429 --> 00:04:00,693
Each tile here is one stretch, lined up by
the month it started.

55
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:05,620
It kept up in 17 of the 715.

56
00:04:05,636 --> 00:04:07,380
That's about 1 in 42.

57
00:04:08,367 --> 00:04:15,167
Every one of those stretches started
between September 1947 and January 1949.

58
00:04:15,207 --> 00:04:18,607
Since then, no starting month has kept up,
including Ruth's.

59
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:23,440
In the typical stretch, prices rose 3.1
percent a year.

60
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:28,960
So after 20 raises, the rising check
typically bought 80.7 percent of what its

61
00:04:29,013 --> 00:04:30,080
first check bought.

62
00:04:30,453 --> 00:04:36,400
In crates, the typical rising check ended
with most of its 10 still lit.

63
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:42,200
A level check, over the same stretches,
typically kept 54.3 percent,

64
00:04:42,253 --> 00:04:43,253
about half.

65
00:04:43,300 --> 00:04:48,680
And typically, the level check had already
sunk to where the rising check would end

66
00:04:48,760 --> 00:04:50,280
by about year 8.

67
00:04:50,467 --> 00:04:55,640
Again, that's buying power against each
check's own start,

68
00:04:55,740 --> 00:04:57,080
not dollars paid.

69
00:04:58,067 --> 00:05:02,227
Even the stretches that ran through the
gentler 2000s and 2010s,

70
00:05:02,247 --> 00:05:05,027
the years closest to Ruth's own, fell
short.

71
00:05:06,033 --> 00:05:09,553
And over 25 years, not one stretch kept
up.

72
00:05:10,567 --> 00:05:15,287
We also ran it on the index Social
Security uses for its yearly raises,

73
00:05:15,319 --> 00:05:18,967
with about the same result; on a gentler
price measure,

74
00:05:18,987 --> 00:05:22,967
the raise kept up more often but still
fell short in most stretches.

75
00:05:23,967 --> 00:05:28,447
Then why did the same raise land so
differently from one start to another?

76
00:05:29,433 --> 00:05:33,193
Ruth isn't the worst case, and she isn't
the best.

77
00:05:33,433 --> 00:05:39,673
Carl, also illustrative, took the same
kind of rising check in January 1966.

78
00:05:39,813 --> 00:05:43,833
His 20 years had the fastest price rise of
any stretch:

79
00:05:43,913 --> 00:05:46,153
6.38 percent a year.

80
00:05:46,281 --> 00:05:50,393
Like Ruth's, his first check bought a row
of 10 crates.

81
00:05:50,606 --> 00:05:55,833
On every one of his 20 anniversaries, his
check bought less than it had the year

82
00:05:55,913 --> 00:05:56,913
before.

83
00:05:56,921 --> 00:05:59,593
Ruth's check climbed back in her early
years;

84
00:05:59,673 --> 00:06:01,193
Carl's never did.

85
00:06:02,900 --> 00:06:09,780
After 20 raises, his check bought 43.1
percent of what his first check bought.

86
00:06:10,140 --> 00:06:14,180
A level check, in his stretch, would have
bought 29 percent.

87
00:06:14,380 --> 00:06:18,660
His raise helped, and it still left him
with less than half.

88
00:06:19,667 --> 00:06:26,667
He wasn't alone: stretches that began in
the 1960s typically ended at 44.3 percent.

89
00:06:27,867 --> 00:06:32,347
Edna, also illustrative, started in
January 1949,

90
00:06:32,367 --> 00:06:36,667
the last starting month when a 2 percent
raise kept up.

91
00:06:36,758 --> 00:06:41,067
Twenty years later, her check still bought
at least what her first one did.

92
00:06:41,267 --> 00:06:46,667
She is part of that early handful, and
nobody who started after her got that

93
00:06:46,713 --> 00:06:47,426
answer.

94
00:06:47,427 --> 00:06:51,067
Her 20 years ended a few years into
Carl's.

95
00:06:51,167 --> 00:06:54,987
The same few years of prices came at the
end of her stretch,

96
00:06:55,019 --> 00:06:59,467
with all 10 crates still lit, and at the
start of his.

97
00:07:00,467 --> 00:07:03,427
And Ruth, at 85, is still losing ground.

98
00:07:03,640 --> 00:07:08,227
In the year to this August, prices rose
3.4 percent,

99
00:07:08,259 --> 00:07:10,067
more than her 2 percent raise.

100
00:07:10,211 --> 00:07:14,227
That's one year of history, not a forecast
for the year after it.

101
00:07:15,233 --> 00:07:19,713
Same raise, same rule: Edna kept up, Ruth
fell short late,

102
00:07:19,753 --> 00:07:21,793
and Carl fell far behind.

103
00:07:22,060 --> 00:07:26,113
In crates, Edna's row ended full, Ruth's
at about 9,

104
00:07:26,233 --> 00:07:28,673
and Carl's at about 4.

105
00:07:28,769 --> 00:07:31,833
What differed was the month each one
started,

106
00:07:31,903 --> 00:07:34,273
and what prices did after that.

107
00:07:35,267 --> 00:07:37,827
So what raise did keep up, in this
history?

108
00:07:38,387 --> 00:07:45,267
A raise of 3 percent a year kept up in
42.4 percent of the 20-year stretches.

109
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:50,627
About 3.1 percent kept up in half: that's
the typical rate from before,

110
00:07:50,827 --> 00:07:53,907
a median of the past, not an expectation.

111
00:07:54,007 --> 00:07:57,667
Keeping up in every stretch took the rate
of Carl's years.

112
00:07:57,800 --> 00:08:02,547
These rates describe what prices did; this
video doesn't say which check,

113
00:08:02,600 --> 00:08:04,707
or which raise, to choose.

114
00:08:05,700 --> 00:08:08,340
The consumer price index is a national
average,

115
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:12,420
not a retiree's own basket, and health
care and housing can weigh differently for

116
00:08:12,460 --> 00:08:13,700
someone Ruth's age.

117
00:08:13,840 --> 00:08:19,940
The 715 stretches overlap, so they're not
715 separate tests.

118
00:08:20,060 --> 00:08:24,500
And because annuity pricing isn't modeled,
nothing here compares the dollars both

119
00:08:24,546 --> 00:08:26,580
checks pay out over a lifetime.

120
00:08:26,720 --> 00:08:31,780
How we know this, and what's left out, is
on this card and in the description.

121
00:08:36,767 --> 00:08:38,607
[thoughtful] This is US only, and it's
history,

122
00:08:38,647 --> 00:08:39,646
not a forecast.

123
00:08:39,647 --> 00:08:44,527
Twenty years ago, Ruth asked whether 2
percent a year would keep up with prices.

124
00:08:44,627 --> 00:08:50,527
Her answer: for 15 years, mostly yes, and
at 85,

125
00:08:50,547 --> 00:08:54,607
her check buys about 9 crates for every 10
her first one bought.

